Amoxicillin

## ADR Assessment
The patient developed rash, itching, nausea and dizziness
approximately three days after starting amoxicillin.

The temporal relationship between initiation of amoxicillin and
the onset of symptoms supports a possible association between
the drug and the reported reaction.

Rash and itching may be suggestive of a hypersensitivity-type
reaction.although the available information is insufficient to
confirm the mechanism Nausea and dizziness may also occur with 
medication use, although other possible causes cannot be excluded 
based on the information provided.

Paracetamol was taken as needed; however, based on the available
timeline, amoxicillin is considered the primary suspected drug.

## Causality Assessment
Based on the available information, the relationship between
amoxicillin and the reported reactions is considered possible.

A definitive causality assessment cannot be established because
information regarding dechallenge, rechallenge, medical history,
exact doses and other potential causes is not available.

## Conclusion
Amoxicillin is the suspected drug associated with the patient's
reported rash, itching, nausea and dizziness. The temporal
relationship between drug exposure and symptom onset supports a
possible drug-related adverse reaction.
